    num: '04',
    title: 'Survival',
    body: 'Both career tracks currently sit outside the highest AI-automation risk zone at the officer/inspector level, because both roles carry legal signing authority, compliance judgment, or customer-facing decision-making that machines are not authorised to perform. The clerical layer underneath both (data entry, basic filing, routine account opening) is the part most exposed to automation and digital self-service over the next decade.',
    note: 'In both tracks, the safer long-term position is the same: aim for the officer/inspector-level role with judgment and signing authority, not the most junior entry-level post, if your score allows a choice.',
  },
];

const sscFitItems = [
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You want variety across departments, not one employer for life',
    body: 'SSC CGL alone can place you in Income Tax, Central Excise, Customs, Audit, CBI, or a dozen other departments depending on your rank and choices. If the idea of a single fixed employer for 30+ years feels limiting, the department-spread appeal of SSC is real.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You prefer fixed hours and slower-paced office process work',
    body: 'Most SSC CGL postings run standard government office hours with five- or six-day weeks and predictable, if sometimes slow, workflows. If sales targets and daily customer pressure sound exhausting rather than energising, this structural difference matters more than salary numbers.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You can accept transfers every few years to different cities',
    body: 'Departmental transfers typically happen every 3-5 years, and some departments allow you to state city or region preferences. If you can plan around this instead of resisting it, SSC postings are workable long-term.',
