];

const bankFitItems = [
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You want a higher starting in-hand salary and faster attempt cycles',
    body: 'Bank PO in-hand pay commonly starts well above SSC CGL\'s typical entry range, and IBPS plus SBI plus RRB together give you more exam cycles per year than SSC\'s single annual CGL notification.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You are comfortable with targets, sales pressure, and customer-facing work',
    body: 'PO roles carry deposit mobilisation, loan disbursal, and cross-selling targets from early in the job, alongside genuine credit and compliance judgment as you get promoted. If performance pressure motivates rather than drains you, this is a real edge.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You can accept rural or semi-urban branch postings in your early years',
    body: 'Branch-based postings, including rural and semi-urban locations, are a structural part of most public sector bank officer roles in the first several years. If you can genuinely plan your life around this rather than treat it as a surprise, banking stays workable.',
  },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Preparing seriously for both tracks at the same time',
    body: 'Quant, reasoning, and English overlap enough that light cross-preparation is fine, but static GK, banking awareness, and each exam\'s specific pattern and difficulty curve are different enough that splitting serious mock-test time across both usually means being mediocre at both instead of strong at one.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Choosing based only on the salary headline you saw once',
    body: 'A screenshot of an SBI PO CTC figure or an SSC CGL Assistant Commissioner\'s eventual pay level is not the entry-level reality. Compare realistic entry-level in-hand pay for the specific post you can actually clear, not the ceiling number from a viral post.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Ignoring the posting-and-lifestyle question until after you clear the exam',
    body: 'Bank branch postings and SSC departmental transfers are structural, not optional extras. Decide before you commit a year of prep whether you can genuinely live with rural branch postings or repeated inter-city transfers, not after you have already invested the time.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Assuming SSC and bank exams are interchangeable "government job" prep',
    body: 'They share a quant-reasoning-English core, but SSC is a multi-department central government recruitment system and banking is a single-sector, single-employer-track system with different awareness sections, different interview weightage, and a different day-to-day job at the end of it.',
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'Not building a backup plan if the first serious attempt does not land',
    body: 'Most successful SSC and bank exam candidates do not clear on their very first attempt. Decide in advance how many attempt cycles you can realistically fund and how you will use the time between attempts to keep a resume-relevant skill or income stream alive.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'SSC vs bank exams which to prepare for in India: which is easier to crack?',
    a: 'Neither is genuinely easy, and the honest comparison depends on what you mean by "easier." SSC CGL usually draws a larger applicant pool (often 25-40 lakh) but its individual cutoffs can be somewhat more forgiving in relative terms. Bank PO exams draw a somewhat smaller applicant pool but set higher individual cutoffs and a faster, more calculation-heavy pace, especially in prelims. Selection ratios for both commonly land in a similar rough zone, roughly 1 in 250-350 depending on the cycle, so treat both as genuinely competitive rather than picking one because it "sounds" easier.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do SSC CGL and bank PO exams share the same syllabus?',
    a: 'They share a strong overlap in Quantitative Aptitude, Reasoning, and English Language basics, so early-stage preparation (the first 2-3 months building fundamentals) genuinely transfers between them. They diverge meaningfully in General Awareness — SSC leans on static GK like history, polity, and science, while bank Mains focuses on banking and financial awareness and current economic affairs — and bank exams add a dedicated Computer Knowledge section that SSC CGL does not test for most posts.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I prepare for both SSC and bank exams at the same time?',
    a: 'You can prepare the shared foundation (quant, reasoning, English) once and use it for both, but trying to run full mock-test cycles and topic-specific prep (static GK for SSC, banking awareness for bank exams) for both tracks in the same window usually spreads your effort too thin. Most successful candidates pick one exam family as the primary target for a given attempt cycle and treat the other as a secondary backup using shared-foundation prep, not a co-equal focus.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which pays more, SSC CGL or bank PO?',
    a: 'At entry level, bank PO roles (IBPS PO and especially SBI PO) commonly report higher in-hand starting pay than most SSC CGL posts — SBI PO in-hand pay is often reported around Rs 80,000-82,000 a month versus a typical SSC CGL in-hand range of roughly Rs 35,000-65,000 a month depending on post and city. Over a full career, SSC posts that lead into departmental promotion ladders (Income Tax, Central Excise) can reach strong senior-level pay too, so the entry-level gap does not automatically predict which path pays more by retirement.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is SSC CGL or bank PO better for work-life balance?',
    a: 'SSC CGL postings generally offer more predictable fixed office hours and fewer performance-target pressures once you are in the role, which many aspirants report as a stronger day-to-day work-life balance. Bank PO roles carry deposit, loan, and cross-selling targets from early on, plus branch-based customer service demands, which can mean longer or less predictable hours, especially around month-end and target periods.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which government exam track has better long-term career growth, SSC or bank exams?',
    a: 'Both offer real promotion ladders, but the shape is different. SSC-linked departmental promotions (for example, Income Tax Inspector to Assistant Commissioner) typically move through seniority plus departmental competitive exams over 7-10+ years. Bank PO promotions inside a public sector bank move through internal merit-based promotion exams and performance reviews, and can move somewhat faster for strong performers, but usually stay within one bank\'s hierarchy rather than opening the department-hopping variety SSC allows.',
